neutrino-production-rate.conceptual_definition:
The neutrino production rate $\dot{q}_{
u}$ is proportional to the product of the neutrino interaction cross-section $\sigma_{
u}$ and the neutrino flux $\Phi_{
u}$, given by $\dot{q}_{
u} \propto \sigma_{
u} \Phi_{
u}$.

neutrino-production-rate.intuitive_summary:
More neutrinos passing through a given area (higher flux $\Phi_{
u}$) combined with a higher probability of interacting with matter (larger cross-section $\sigma_{
u}$) directly results in a higher rate of neutrino production or interaction events ($\dot{q}_{
u}$).

neutrino-production-rate.interpretation:
The term $\dot{q}_{
u}$ represents the neutrino production or interaction rate per unit volume and time, measured in $\text{m}^{-3}\text{s}^{-1}$. The parameter $\sigma_{
u}$ is the neutrino interaction cross-section representing the effective target area for interactions, measured in $\text{m}^2$. The variable $\Phi_{
u}$ denotes the neutrino flux, which is the number of neutrinos passing through a unit area per unit time, quantified in $\text{m}^{-2}\text{s}^{-1}$.

neutrino-production-rate.limits_and_boundary:
In the limit where the neutrino flux $\Phi_{
u} \to 0$, the production rate vanishes ($\dot{q}_{
u} \to 0$). Similarly, as the cross-section $\sigma_{
u} \to 0$ for weakly interacting sterile states, the production rate becomes negligible.
